        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Small-Dog Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines, and we review treadmills for people rather than for dogs. The
            picks were made from dealer and retailer listings, the makers’ own pages where they loaded, and
            independent coverage, all checked on 5 November 2026. Four filters did the work.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Built for dogs.</strong> Every machine here is sold as a dog treadmill, with a slow
            starting speed and, on most, side panels that keep a small dog centred on the belt. A human <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking pad</a> can work for some small dogs under close supervision, as our guide to <a href="/treadmill-for-dogs/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dogs on a human treadmill</a> explains, but a dog machine is designed around a dog’s height, stride and nerves.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A stated running length.</strong> A machine qualified only if its seller gives the
            length of the running surface, because that is what decides whether it suits your dog. Many
            marketplace “small dog treadmills” give a weight limit and an overall size but not the belt, and
            we left those out.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Available, or honestly not.</strong> Small-dog treadmills come in and out of stock, and
            several familiar ones were unavailable on the day. The <strong>dogPACER</strong> range, which
            heads our general roundup, had disappeared from Canine Athletes’ store, where the product pages
            for the LF 3.1, LF 4.0 and the small-dog <strong>MiniPacer</strong> no longer opened; Ray Allen
            Manufacturing’s MiniPacer link led to a general treadmills category; and dogpacer.com returned a
            server error. A US marketplace copy of the LF 3.1 listing dated May 2026 still showed it in stock,
            and our large-dog guide reported in October that an Australian dealer referred to the closure of
            dogPACER in the US, which we could not confirm with the company. GoPet’s smallest folding model,
            the <strong>PR710F</strong> (a 42 by 16-inch surface for dogs up to 88 lb, by its dealers’
            listings), had been removed from two dealers that sold it. We kept the PawRunner, which could not
            be confirmed in stock, because it is the right shape for small dogs and a buyer will meet it; its
            card says so and carries no price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Searched for recalls.</strong> We searched the CPSC recall database for every brand here.
            None has recalled a dog treadmill. Costway has recalled high chairs and, with Giantex, portable
            steam saunas; Lifepro Fitness recalled <a href="/best-home-saunas/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>infrared sauna</a> blankets in October 2025. Neither recall
            concerns these machines, but you should know who you are buying from.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing-small-dogs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Sizing: Running Length Against Your Dog’s Length</h2>
<p>
            The rule of thumb used across the category, and stated in Whole Dog Journal’s January 2026
            comparison, is that the running surface should be at least twice the dog’s length from the tip of
            the nose to the base of the tail. That lets the dog extend fully without stepping off the back.
            WDJ notes that a dog on a deck that is too short can become nervous about slipping off the end.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Measure your dog.</strong> Stand your dog square on a flat floor and measure from nose tip
            to the base of the tail, not the tip. Double it. That is the minimum running length to look for.
            Small breeds vary more than you might expect: a long-backed Dachshund or Corgi can need more belt
            than a heavier but compact terrier.
          </p>
          <p>The arithmetic, for the machines on this page:</p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Your dog, nose to base of tail</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Running length needed (2x)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Machines here that pass</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Up to 14 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">28 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">All five</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15 to 19 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">30 to 38 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">GYMAX 2 HP and Costway (39 in.), PawRunner, PR720F, Tracks</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 to 21 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">40 to 42 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">PawRunner (42 in. by WDJ’s figure), PR720F, Tracks</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">22 to 26 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">44 to 52 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">PR720F and Tracks (about 52 in.)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Over 26 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Over 52 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None; see the general or large-dog roundups</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Ignore the weight rating as a guide to size.</strong> Small-dog machines often carry weight
            ratings far beyond the dogs they suit. Costway rates its 39-inch machine for 220 lb, and Lifepro
            markets the PawRunner for dogs up to 130 lb, which Whole Dog Journal calls a little misleading; it
            judges the PawRunner suitable for dogs under about 25 to 30 lb with short strides. The weight
            rating tells you the belt will not stall under your dog. The running length tells you whether your
            dog fits.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Width.</strong> Sixteen inches, as on the GoPets and the GYMAX by its listing, is plenty for a toy or small dog to move
            without placing its feet in a narrow line. Narrower belts work for very small dogs but leave less
            margin for a dog that drifts.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our explainer on <a href="/dog-treadmill-vs-human-treadmill/#belt-length" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt length against a dog’s stride</a> covers the same rule for every size of dog.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed-and-controls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Speed, Side Panels and Controls for a Small Dog</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A low, fine-grained bottom speed matters most.</strong> Small dogs take short, quick steps,
            and their comfortable walking pace on a belt is slow. Most machines here start at around half a
            mile an hour; GoPet has given the PR720F’s bottom speed as 1 mph. What varies is how finely the speed can be stepped up from there, and that is the
            figure we would most like makers to publish. A machine that jumps in large steps is harder to
            introduce to a nervous dog.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Top speed matters least.</strong> The PR700’s top speed is 8 mph, the GYMAX 2 HP’s 7.5 mph and the
            PawRunner’s is listed variously as 7.5 and 12 mph. No small dog needs any of those speeds on a
            treadmill, and a fast top speed is not a feature for a Chihuahua. Treat it as headroom and ignore
            it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Side panels.</strong> Panels along both sides keep a small dog centred and stop it stepping
            sideways off the belt. The GoPets are built with them; the GYMAX 2 HP’s are removable,
            because its machine converts to a walking pad for a person. For a small dog, keep them on.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A remote or a stop you can reach.</strong> You should be standing beside the dog, not
            reaching across it for a console. A remote control, or a stop button on the side you stand,
            lets you slow or stop the belt the moment the dog hesitates. Most machines here have one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Noise.</strong> Small dogs can be wary of motor noise. Makers describe all of these as
            quiet, which tells you little; GYMAX and Costway give a figure of under 45 dB, which we have not measured.
            Introduce the machine switched off first, then switched on with the belt still, before the belt
            ever moves under the dog.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motorized-or-slatmill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Motorised Treadmill or Slatmill for a Small Dog?</h2>
<p>
            A <strong>motorized dog treadmill</strong> moves the belt for the dog at a speed you set. A <strong>slatmill</strong> has no motor: the belt of slats moves only when the dog drives it, and
            stops when the dog stops. Our guide to <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual against motorised treadmills</a> covers the same trade-off for people.
          </p>
          <p>
            For small dogs, the choice is mostly made for you. Slatmills are built for athletic medium and
            large dogs that want to run, and they are expensive: Dog Trotter USA’s smallest Classic slatmill,
            the Medium, has a 12-inch-wide belt with a 49-inch safe running zone for dogs up to 70 lb, and
            Canine Athletes listed the Classic from $2,900 on 5 November 2026. That is a machine for a Border
            Collie or a Malinois, not a Pomeranian. If you are handy, our guide to <a href="/how-to-build-a-dog-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">building a dog treadmill</a> covers DIY slatmills, which some owners scale down for small dogs.
          </p>
          <p>
            A motorised treadmill suits most small dogs better. It suits the slow, steady walking that most
            small-dog owners want on a rainy day or for weight management, and it suits structured,
            vet-directed programmes, where a set speed and a set time are the point. The main hazard of a
            powered belt, that the dog cannot stop it, is managed by supervision, a remote and never tethering,
            which the safety section below covers.
          </p>
          <p>
            Rehabilitation is a different matter. If your vet has suggested treadmill work after surgery or an
            injury, the programme should come from the vet or a canine rehabilitation therapist, and many
            clinics offer <a href="/underwater-treadmill-for-dogs/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">underwater treadmill sessions</a>,
            which take weight off the joints in a way no home machine can.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="small-dog-health" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Small Dogs, Puppies and Older Dogs: Talk to the Vet First</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill is a tool for exercise a dog would otherwise miss, not a replacement for walks. A walk
            gives a dog smells, other dogs, changes of surface and direction, and time with you; a belt gives
            it steady movement in one direction. Most trainers and vets who use treadmills use them alongside
            walks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ask your vet before starting if your dog is a puppy, is elderly, is overweight, has a heart or
            breathing condition, or has had joint, back or knee problems. Small breeds have their own
            vulnerabilities: flat-faced breeds such as Pugs and French Bulldogs overheat and struggle for breath
            more easily, long-backed breeds such as Dachshunds are prone to back problems, and many toy breeds
            are prone to kneecap problems. None of that rules out a treadmill. It does mean the speed, the
            session length and whether to use any incline should come from someone who has examined your dog.
          </p>
          <p>
            Start with very short sessions, a few minutes at a slow walk, and build up over weeks. Watch your
            dog rather than the console. Stop if the dog pants heavily, lags to the back of the belt, tries
            repeatedly to get off, limps, or seems distressed. Small dogs heat up quickly in a warm room, so
            keep water available and sessions short in summer.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most small-dog treadmills offer a little incline. Leave it flat unless your vet or rehabilitation
            therapist recommends otherwise; incline loads the hind legs and back, which is useful in a
            programme and unhelpful by accident.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Safety: Never Tether, Always Supervise</h2>
<p>
            Two rules matter more than anything else on this page.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Never tether a dog to a treadmill.</strong> Some dog treadmills are sold with a tethering
            arm or anchor point, and some sellers describe clipping the lead to the machine. Do not do it. A
            tethered dog cannot step off if it stumbles, panics or tires, and on a moving belt that can mean
            being dragged. A small dog is easily hurt that way. A dog should always be free to step off. If it
            will only stay on when tied, it is not ready, and the answer is more patient training, not a
            shorter lead.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Never leave a dog alone on a treadmill.</strong> Stay beside the dog for the whole
            session, with the remote or the stop in your hand. Never leave the room with the belt moving, and
            unplug or remove the key when the machine is not in use, so a curious dog, or a child, cannot start
            it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Introduce it slowly.</strong> Let the dog explore the machine switched off, with treats.
            Then switch it on with the belt still. Then lead the dog on at the lowest speed with you beside it,
            for seconds rather than minutes, and reward calm walking. Expect this to take several short
            sessions; some dogs take weeks, and a few never accept a belt. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-for-dogs/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">training a dog to use a treadmill</a> sets out the steps.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the machine for the dog.</strong> If you also use a human treadmill in the same room,
            keep the dog away from it while you run. Belts and rollers catch paws, tails and fur. The same
            applies to the dog’s own machine when someone else is using it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our wider <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety guide</a> covers keys, cords and the space to leave behind a belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-and-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Space, Delivery and the Terms You Sign Up To</h2>
<p>
            Small-dog treadmills are compact by treadmill standards, but they are not small furniture. The
            PR700 measures about 46 by 23 inches and weighs about 63 lb, by Canine Athletes’ listing; Costway
            gives its machine as about 47 by 20 inches. Measure the space, leave room behind the belt for the
            dog to step off, and leave room beside it for you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Read the returns terms before ordering.</strong> They vary more than the machines do.
            Canine Athletes says GoPet treadmills are not eligible for return once shipped and charges a $300
            cancellation fee, and they ship by LTL freight direct from the maker; FactoryPure ships the PR700
            direct from the maker as well, and sells <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>extended warranties</a>. Costway offers 30-day returns on its
            own store for its version; for the GYMAX, check the Amazon listing’s return terms. If your dog may refuse the belt, and some do, a seller that accepts returns is worth
            paying more for.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the warranty.</strong> GoPet’s is one year, according to both dealers. Costway gives
            365 days. Target’s listing for the PawRunner states a lifetime limited warranty, which we could not
            read on Lifepro’s own site. A dog treadmill’s motor and belt work in short sessions at low speed,
            which is gentle on them, but a one-year term is short for a machine costing several hundred
            dollars.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Freight means kerbside.</strong> LTL freight delivery usually means the driver brings the
            box to the kerb, not inside. Arrange help to move it. Our <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill delivery guide</a> explains what freight delivery includes and what to check before you sign.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Treadmill for a Small Dog</h2>
          <p>Small dogs are easier to fit than large ones, which makes it easy to skip the steps that matter.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying by the weight rating</h3>
              <p>A small-dog machine rated for 130 lb or 220 lb tells you nothing about whether your dog fits. Measure nose to base of tail, double it, and compare that with the running surface. A long-backed small dog can need more belt than a heavier compact one.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ordering from a seller that will not take it back</h3>
              <p>Some dogs never accept a belt. Several specialist sellers charge cancellation fees and accept no returns once a treadmill ships. Read the terms first, and for a first machine consider a seller with a 30-day return window.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Starting too fast, or too long</h3>
              <p>A small dog’s comfortable belt speed is very slow, and its first sessions should be measured in seconds. Introduce the machine switched off, then still, then at the lowest speed with you beside it, and stop while the dog is still calm.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Tethering the dog or leaving the room</h3>
              <p>A small dog tied to a moving belt cannot step off if it slips, and a dog left alone has no one to stop it. Never tether, never leave a dog on a moving belt, keep the remote in your hand, and remove the key when you are done.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            For a toy breed, the <strong class="text-white">GoPet PetRun PR700</strong> is the machine to buy:
            a 28 by 16-inch running surface, speeds from 0.6 mph and side panels, in stock at $590 from two US
            dealers (Amazon lists it too, for more). Longer small dogs need the 52-inch GoPet PR720F from a
            dealer, if you can find it, or the 41-inch GYMAX on a budget, and the <strong class="text-white">GYMAX 2 HP</strong> machine is the budget option at about $180 on
            Amazon.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever you choose, measure your dog against the running surface first, speak to your vet before the
            first session, and never tether or leave a dog on a moving belt. For bigger dogs, see our <a href="/best-dog-treadmills-for-large-dogs/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">large-dog guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

