        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What size treadmill does a small dog need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Measure your dog from the tip of the nose to the base of the tail and double it. That is the minimum running length under the widely used rule of thumb, so a dog measuring 14 inches needs about 28 inches and a 20-inch Dachshund about 40 inches. Ignore the weight rating as a size guide; small-dog machines are often rated for far heavier dogs than they fit.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best doggie treadmill for small dogs?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For toy breeds, the GoPet PetRun PR700, with a 28 by 16-inch running surface and speeds from 0.6 mph, was in stock at $590 from two US dealers on 5 November 2026. Amazon also lists the PR700, at a higher price. For longer small dogs, GoPet’s PR720F, at about 52 inches, is the step up if you can find it in stock at a dealer. On a budget, GYMAX’s 2 HP small-dog machine was about $180 on Amazon, and GYMAX’s longer 243 lb model about $190.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can a small dog use a human treadmill or walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Some can, at a slow walk and under close supervision, because a small dog needs less belt length. But human machines have no side panels, start faster than many small dogs like and have controls out of your reach while you stand beside the dog. A dog treadmill is designed around those problems. Our guide to dogs on human treadmills sets out the rules if you try.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a motorized dog treadmill safe for a small dog?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be, with supervision. Never tether the dog, never leave it alone on a moving belt, start at the lowest speed with you beside it and the remote in your hand, and stop at the first sign of distress. Ask your vet first, especially for puppies, older dogs, flat-faced breeds and dogs with back or knee problems.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the dogPACER MiniPacer still available?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              We could not find it in stock from a US seller on 5 November 2026. Canine Athletes’ product page for it no longer opened, Ray Allen’s link led to a general category page, and dogpacer.com returned a server error. A marketplace copy of a dogPACER listing from May 2026 still showed the full-size LF 3.1 in stock. Check with a seller before relying on any dogPACER model.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long should a small dog walk on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Start with a few minutes at a slow walk and build up over several weeks, watching the dog rather than the clock. Your vet can suggest a target for your dog’s age, breed and health. A treadmill session adds to walks rather than replacing them.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

